      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{8}{10 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the linear substitution $u=\\sqrt{10} x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\sqrt{10},\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{8}{10 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8\\int \\frac{1}{1+(\\sqrt{10} x)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 8(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10})\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{4 \\sqrt{10}}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{4 \\sqrt{10} \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(u \\right)}}{5}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{4 \\sqrt{10} \\operatorname{arctan}{\\left(\\sqrt{10} x \\right)}}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
